    [
     1,
     "Timothée,<div><br></div><div>Based upon previous examples I think you just need to add the following to your code:</div><div><br></div><div>+ labs(colour = &quot;legend title&quot;)<br><br></div><div>So...</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div><br></div>\n\n<div>gplot(cin,aes(x=t)) + </div><div>geom_line(aes(y=re,colour=&#39;Enemy richness&#39;)) + </div><div>geom_line(aes(y=rv,colour=&#39;Victim richness&#39;)) + </div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div>facet_grid(Ar~Ur) + </div><div>labs(colour = &quot;legend title&quot;)</div>\n\n<div><br></div><div>Never seen an example with a facet though so give it a go and report back. </div><div><br></div><div>Brandon</div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2010/8/7 Timothée Poisot <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">tpo...@um2.fr</a>&gt;</span><br>\n\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"></blockquote></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\">Dear all,<div><br></div><div>I am quite new with ggplot2, that I find more intuitive than lattice, and I ran into a problem with the automatic generation of legend</div>\n\n<div><br></div><div>I want to plot two (or more) variables that are all in the same dataframe (called cin), which I do using :</div><div><br></div><div>ggplot(cin,aes(x=t)) + geom_line(aes(y=re,colour=&#39;Enemy richness&#39;)) + geom_line(aes(y=rv,colour=&#39;Victim richness&#39;)) + facet_grid(Ar~Ur)</div>\n\n<div><br></div><div>The result is<br><div>\n<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><span style=\"border-collapse:separate;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&#39;Lucida Grande&#39;;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;font-weight:normal;letter-spacing:normal;line-height:normal;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;white-space:normal;word-spacing:0px;font-size:medium\"><span style=\"font-size:11px\"><div>\n\n<font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><br><img height=\"385\" width=\"458\" src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/ggplot2/attach/3bfc9ec9d71f9e0d/Capture%20d%E2%80%99%C3%A9cran%202010-08-07%20%C3%A0%2017.43.42.png?part=0.1\"></span></font></div><div><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">My problem is the title of the legend. I am sure there is a way to specificy it, and my naive guess would be that I need to add an option in the first &#39;aes&#39; the one within ggplot)?</span></font></div>\n\n<div><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><br></span></font></div><div><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">Can anybody help with this?</span></font></div><div><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><br>\n\n</span></font></div><div><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">t</span></font></div><div><font size=\"3\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><br></span></font></div><div